//
// A fn-ref that captures only SCALARS can have its minted return lifted: nothing in the
// closure holds a store, so nothing the callee hands back can be borrowing from one
// (loft#1248).
//
// `callref_captures` gates the lift, and it asked *"does this fn-ref have ANY capture"*.  What
// makes a capture dangerous is that it HOLDS A STORE — the hazard the gate exists for is a
// returned value that borrows from the enclosing scope — so a captured integer buys the
// decline nothing and costs one leaked store per call.  The fn-ref's deps name the closure
// RECORD rather than the captured variables, so the question is asked one level in: the
// record's FIELDS are the captures, and `is_dbref` is the one home for whether a field holds a
// store.
//
// ⚠ `is_dbref` answers the LAYOUT question — *"does this value occupy a DbRef slot?"* — and the
// invariant above is stated in terms of BORROWING, which is the reach question its sibling
// `holds_dbref` answers (tuple-transparent, since a tuple occupies no DbRef slot but reaches
// one through its elements).  The two disagree on exactly one capture shape, and it is
// reachable: a captured `(integer, vector<integer>)` reads `is_dbref=false, holds_dbref=true`,
// so the gate treats it as harmless.  That is measured SAFE rather than lucky-so-far, and the
// cell below is why: the shapes where a return could actually borrow from such a capture --
// returning the captured vector, or a struct holding it -- never reach this gate at all, so
// the only tuple captures that get here are the ones whose return is a fresh mint.  Widening
// to `holds_dbref` would decline those and buy back the leak for no hazard.  If a future change
// lets a borrow-shaped return reach this gate, this is the predicate to revisit first.
//
// THE AXIS IS CAPTURE + A MINTED RETURN, and not the `??` discharge the issue was filed
// against.  Measured at N=400 before this change: a closure over an INTEGER returning a fresh
// struct leaked identically to the filed repro with no discharge anywhere in it, while a
// capturing lambda that RETURNS its capture was flat — nothing minted, so nothing is handed
// up.  Both of those are cells here.
//
// ⚠ THE FLAT CELLS BELOW ARE ONLY FLAT ON A TREE CARRYING loft#1245.  Before that fix the
// non-capturing lambda leaks too (measured 404 at N=400 on `main` and on #1245's parent), so
// re-deriving this file's axis on `main` finds every contrast cell leaking and concludes the
// report is misfiled.  A differential claim's contrast cell carries its base, and this is that
// base: read these numbers against a post-#1245 build or not at all.
//
// @falsified-at: f70862b3a — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0, native panicked ->
//   clean.  On the control build `test_a_scalar_capture_does_not_leak` aborts partway through
//   its loop, and the native channel names where: "panicked at src/database/allocation.rs",
//   which is the u16 store table exhausting at 65535 live stores.  The other three cells pass
//   on both builds, which is what makes them controls rather than a second copy of the guard.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.

struct P1248 { n: integer }

fn test_a_scalar_capture_does_not_leak() {
  // The cell this change fixes.  No `??`, no nullable, nothing store-backed in the closure —
  // just an integer read from the enclosing frame and a freshly minted return.  A build that
  // declines the lift on the mere PRESENCE of a capture holds one store per call and dies at
  // the u16 store ceiling well before this loop ends.
  m = 7;
  g = fn(k: integer) -> P1248 { P1248 { n: m } };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..70000 { s += g(0).n; }
  assert(s == 490000, "70000 calls each answering the captured 7, got {s}");
}

fn test_a_capture_that_mints_nothing_is_unaffected() {
  // Flat before and after: the callee returns the CAPTURE, so nothing was minted and nothing
  // is handed up.  This is half of what names the axis — capture alone is not the condition.
  cap: P1248 = P1248 { n: 3 };
  g = fn(k: integer) -> P1248 { cap };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..70000 { s += g(0).n; }
  assert(s == 210000, "70000 calls each answering the captured struct, got {s}");
}

fn test_capturing_nothing_is_unaffected() {
  // The other half: minting alone is not the condition either.  loft#1245 made this flat, and
  // it is the cell whose base the header warns about.
  g = fn(k: integer) -> P1248 { P1248 { n: 5 } };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..70000 { s += g(0).n; }
  assert(s == 350000, "70000 calls each minting a fresh 5, got {s}");
}

fn test_a_store_backed_capture_still_declines_and_still_answers() {
  // The conservative half, deliberately NOT a ceiling cell: a closure over a store-backed
  // capture that MINTS still declines the lift and still leaks one store per call, which is
  // loft#1248's filed repro and is not closed here.  The witness it needs exists — the
  // runtime's `minted_here` already separates a minted return from a borrowed one — and the
  // open work is releasing at STATEMENT scope rather than at frame scope.
  //
  // What this cell holds is that narrowing the gate did not change the ANSWER on that side:
  // the value is still right, and `LOFT_POISON=1` reports no use-after-free, which is the
  // failure mode a too-wide narrowing would produce.
  none: P1248? = null;
  g = fn(k: integer) -> P1248 { none ?? P1248 { n: 5 } };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..200 { s += g(0).n; }
  assert(s == 1000, "the discharging closure still answers 5 each time, got {s}");
}

fn test_a_tuple_capture_holding_a_store_is_allowed_and_answers_right() {
  // The cell where the gate's two candidate predicates disagree.  `pair` is a TUPLE, so it
  // occupies no DbRef slot and `is_dbref` reads false -- while the vector inside it means the
  // capture does reach a store, which is what `holds_dbref` reports.  The gate takes the
  // layout reading and allows the lift.
  //
  // Both halves are asserted, because "it did not crash" is not the claim: the loop reads
  // THROUGH the captured tuple's store element on every call, and the enclosing `pv` must
  // still be intact afterwards.  A gate that wrongly adopted the capture's store would free a
  // live variable, which is loft#1114's shape and what `LOFT_POISON=1` reports here as clean.
  pv = [10, 20, 30];
  pair = (7, pv);
  g = fn(k: integer) -> P1248 { P1248 { n: pair.1[0] ?? -1 } };
  s = 0;
  for _ in 0..200 { s += g(0).n; }
  assert(s == 2000, "200 calls each reading 10 through the captured tuple, got {s}");
  assert((pv[0] ?? -1) == 10, "the captured vector is intact after the loop: {pv[0] ?? -1}");
}
